LowestBreakIteration, as shown in the following code:
var numbers = Enumerable.Range(1, 1000);
Parallel.ForEach(numbers, (i, parallelLoopState) =>
{
Console.WriteLine($"For i={i} LowestBreakIteration =
{parallelLoopState.LowestBreakIteration} and
Task id ={Task.CurrentId}");
if (i >= 10)
{
parallelLoopState.Break();
}
});
When we run the preceding code in Visual Studio, we get the following output:
Here, we run the code on a multi-core processor. As you can see, a lot of iterations get a
null value for LowestBreakIteration as the code is being executed on multiple cores. On
iteration 17, one core calls the Break() method and sets the value of
LowestBreakIteration to 17. On iteration 10, another core calls Break() and further
reduces the number to 10. Later, on iteration 9, another core calls Break(), and further
reduces the number to 9.
